    "lawCode": "Add Art 2-D §88, Bank L",
    "lawSection": "Banking Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S5624\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the banking law, in relation to community bank service\ncorporations\n\nPURPOSE:\n\nTo permit community banks to invest in and utilize service\ncorporations to achieve economies of scale when complying with\nregulations.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 - state that this act shall be known as the \"Community Bank\nService Corporation Act\"\n\nSection 2 - amends the Banking Law by adding a new article 2-D,\n\"Community Bank Service Corporations\", Section 88 of which outlines\nthe authorized activities and investment therein of such corporations.\n\nSection 3 - specifies enactment sixty days after it becomes law.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nCommunity banks have significant regulatory burdens. Banking laws and\nregulations are based on a \"one size fits all\" approach. The\nconsolidation of banks in the community bank sector is in large\nmeasure driven by economies of scale when implementing regulations.\nThese regulatory costs are more easily absorbed by larger banks.\nCompliance costs are significant for the Bank Secrecy Act, anti-money\nlaundering and information technology requirements. In some cases it\nmight be possible for community banks to work collectively to provide\nservices using another bank, third party vendor or jointly undertaking\nthe performance of such services.\n\nThe community bank service corporation is a vehicle which would permit\ncommunity banks to use such corporation to provide shared services\namong member banks. This approach would enable community banks to\nachieve more scale when responding to regulatory requirements. For\nexample, a single information technology platform which would serve\nmultiple member financial institutions could provide savings.\n\nUnder banking law § 235-d, there is currently authority to form\nservice corporations for savings banks. This authority was added to\nbanking law in 1983. This legislative proposal would expand this\nconcept to all community banks, including federal and state chartered\nbanks and thrifts under $8 billion dollars.\n\nThis bill is directed at providing community banks with an option for\nmeeting compliance requirements. The objective is to preserve\ncommunity banks in New York State which play an important role in\nsmall business and agricultural loans.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n\nNew Bill\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone to the State\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nSixtieth day after it has become law\n\n",
